WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.590
Star Wars: The Last Jedi has come and gone.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.259
The credits have rolled, the battles have
been fought, and there was more family drama

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:09.130
than you could shake a porg at.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:13.230
But now that the movie's over, you've probably
got more than a few thoughts about just what

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.230
it all means.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:17.850
Well, think no more: we're about to explain
the ending of The Last Jedi.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.200
But before we begin, one last word of warning:
major spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.320
A Forceful conclusion

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.090
We got a glimpse at all kinds of Force-powered
shenanigans this time around.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.720
From Rey and Kylo Ren staying up late to chat
on the Force-phone, to Snoke waving Rey around

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.300
like a rag doll, the Force has never gotten
used quite like this.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.000
General Leia strapped on Force-fueled rocket
boosters and flew herself through actual outer

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.000
space!

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.230
That beats the hell out of Qui-Gon Jinn messing
around with a bunch of stupid dice.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.440
But the true expression of the Force in this
movie came in the form of Luke Skywalker projecting

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:55.410
an illusion of himself across the galaxy to
fool Kylo Ren during their duel on Crait.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.930
And when the fight was over and the Resistance
escaped, we saw Luke just … disappear.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.190
If you're new to this whole Star Wars thing,
you're going to want to check out a little

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.650
film called A New Hope.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:10.350
In that movie, Darth Vader swings his lightsaber
right through Luke's master, Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:15.500
But instead of making a Kenobi-kabob, Obi-Wan
simply disappears, his robes falling to the

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.500
floor.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.900
The same thing happened when Yoda passed away
in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.450
In both instances, the Jedi masters became
one with the Force, ultimately helping Luke

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.450
on his quest.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.480
Now that Luke has pulled the same trick, it's
fair to say Rey will reap the benefits in

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.850
the same way going forward.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.350
And it's a safe bet he'll show up as a Force
ghost in Episode 9, just like his little green

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.490
buddy did this time around.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.670
Suffering from a Phasma attack

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:43.600
In an interesting twist, we got the return
of Captain Phasma, one of the most underutilized

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.360
characters from The Force Awakens.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.020
She makes her return in The Last Jedi and
gets into a pretty neat fight with Finn.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:54.510
And then he hits her in the face and she falls
down into a bunch of fire and disintegrates

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:55.440
or whatever.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.010
It's impossible to know for sure, but it's
tough to imagine that Phasma will come back

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.880
for a third time in Episode 9.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.990
The explosion she fell into looked pretty
gnarly overall, so even though she was wearing

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.340
all that fancy armor, she's probably gone
off to that big moisture farm in the sky.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.280
In fact, now we know for sure just what role
Phasma plays in this new Star Wars trilogy:

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.530
she's the new Boba Fett.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.260
You know, because she looks cool, seems important
— and then sucks real bad right before falling

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.280
into a hole and biting the big one in a lame,
pathetic way.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.440
Bye, Captain Phasma.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.980
Too bad you never got to, like, do anything.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.860
Snoke and mirrors

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.890
Rumors about Snoke's true identity have been
floating around ever since he first appeared

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.459
in The Force Awakens, and we didn't get any
answers to that question here.

00:02:52.459 --> 00:02:56.600
And despite getting sliced into chunks in
The Last Jedi, it's still possible for him

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.410
to pull things together and come back in the
next movie.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.670
For starters, fans have long speculated that
Snoke is actually Darth Plagueis — the Sith

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.420
Lord who supposedly found a way to cheat death
itself.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:11.200
The rumor became so inescapable that it's
even gotten several official denials.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.069
But even if he's not Plagueis, if you look
at his face in the movie, it sure looks like

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:18.570
he's been cut up and put back together more
than a few times already.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.069
Whoever he really is, he might be only mostly
dead.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:26.650
Besides, there's ample evidence that being
hacked up with a lightsaber isn't as debilitating

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:27.650
as you might think.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.300
The legend of Plagueis is one example.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.390
Darth Maul was chopped in half, just like
Snoke, and fell down a giant hole in The Phantom

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:37.610
Menace…and he came back in the comics and
cartoons with robo-legs.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.860
And what about Darth Vader?

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.370
He was chopped into bits, and burned to a
crisp by an actual volcano, and he still stuck

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.650
around for a bunch of movies after a quick
Hot Topic makeover.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.030
This may not be the last we've seen of Snoke
at all…

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:51.830
The next Star War

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.500
Whether it's Ren who's in charge or some glued-together
Snoke, the First Order definitely has more

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.080
going for it than the Resistance, which is
down to its last members.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.739
But the end of the film definitely implies
that the legend of Luke Skywalker is spreading.

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:08.739
Needless to say, the escape from Crait isn't
a victory for the Resistance, but it's likely

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:11.940
more than enough to spark some new recruits
to the rebel cause.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.090
Poe's finally learned a thing or two about
how to be a leader, Finn has clear ideas about

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:20.789
when to fight and when to run, and Rey is
tapping into her true power, so maybe the

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:23.770
Resistance is actually in a better position
than it may seem.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:28.009
One of the main ideas behind The Last Jedi
is that things are not always how they appear.

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:32.749
Luke fooled Ren, Ren fooled Rey, and Disney
fooled all of us into caring about Porgs.

00:04:32.749 --> 00:04:37.439
"Porgs, which no one has actually seen in
action yet, but which will become an internet

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.540
phenomenon."

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.159
In the end, Rey had the right idea when she
said that the galaxy needs a legend—the

00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:50.580
Battle of Crait may actually serve as a surprising
rallying cry for the Resistance to beef up

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.099
its ranks for the final showdown with the
First Order in Episode 9.

00:04:54.099 --> 00:04:56.270
We'll just have to wait and see.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:58.139
Rey's origins

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:02.610
Building off of that theme of deception and
legends, we got a pretty anticlimactic answer

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.889
to one of the most burning questions from
The Force Awakens: who are Rey's parents?

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.840
Episode 7 heavily implied that Han Solo knew
something about her...

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.420
"Who's the girl?"

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:21.369
Well, if Kylo Ren is to be believed, they're
a couple of nobodies who sold her for drinking

00:05:21.369 --> 00:05:22.369
money.

00:05:22.369 --> 00:05:26.289
Only, we've learned pretty conclusively that
Kylo Ren is not the most truthful guy.

00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:30.259
He tricked Rey into turning herself over to
the First Order so she could turn him to the

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:31.259
Light.

00:05:31.259 --> 00:05:35.650
Convincing Rey she's not actually important—except
to him as his partner in galactic conquest—falls

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.800
right in line with his overall goals and deceptive
character.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.169
Just as Snoke might be able to pull himself
together in time for the final chapter in

00:05:42.169 --> 00:05:46.809
this saga, it's also plausible that the subject
of Rey's mysterious parentage isn't quite

00:05:46.809 --> 00:05:51.509
as closed as Kylo Ren—and writer-director
Rian Johnson—might want us to believe.

00:05:51.509 --> 00:05:52.509
And who knows?

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:53.680
Maybe her mom is Captain Phasma!

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:56.999
Thanks for watching!

00:05:56.999 --> 00:05:59.379
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:59.379 --> 00:06:01.659
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

